course: world
lesson: 119
title: The Moon's month and the year round the Sun
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Two movements give us our biggest everyday units of time. The Earth spins once a day, which gives day and night, and it travels once round the Sun in about 365 and a quarter days, which gives the year and its seasons. A 10-year-old has done 10 laps of the Sun, each about 940 million kilometres long.
explain: The Moon makes no light of its own: the Sun always lights half of it, and as the Moon travels round the Earth we see more or less of that lit half. From new moon, when the lit side faces away and we see almost nothing, it waxes (grows) to full moon and wanes (shrinks) back again in about 29 and a half days. That was the first "month", the "moonth".
explain: Twelve Moon months make about 354 days, 11 short of a year, so most years have 12 full moons and some have 13. An extra one is called a "blue moon", though it isn't blue, and that mismatch is why every calendar must choose: the Moon, the Sun, or both.
explain: The Moon rises about 50 minutes later each night, because while the Earth spins, the Moon moves on along its path and the Earth has to turn a bit more to catch it up. So the Moon's 12 or so hours in the sky slide round the clock, and you often see it in the daytime too.
explain: The Moon spins once in the same time it takes to go round the Earth, so the same face always looks at us, like a dancer turning to keep facing a partner. We never see its far side from Earth.
fact: A day is one spin of the Earth; a year is one trip of the Earth round the Sun, about 365 and a quarter days.
fact: Each trip of the Earth round the Sun is about 940 million kilometres long.
fact: The Moon makes no light of its own; it shines because sunlight falls on it.
fact: From one full moon to the next takes about 29 and a half days, so after a full moon on 3 March the next is around 1 or 2 April.
fact: At new moon the lit side of the Moon faces away from us, so we see almost nothing for about a night.
fact: A Moon that looks bigger each night is waxing; a Moon that looks smaller each night is waning.
fact: Twelve Moon months make about 354 days, about 11 days less than a year of 365 days.
fact: 365 ÷ 29.5 is about 12.4, so most years have 12 full moons and some have 13.
fact: An extra full moon is called a blue moon; it is not blue, and "once in a blue moon" means very rarely.
fact: A supermoon is a full moon when the Moon is nearest the Earth; it looks only a little bigger than the smallest full moon, hardly enough to notice.
fact: The Moon rises on average about 50 minutes later each night.
fact: The Moon can be seen in daylight when it is bright enough and not too close to the Sun in the sky.
fact: The Moon spins once in the same time it goes round the Earth, about 27 days, so the same side always faces us.
fact: People long ago counted months by the Moon for farming and festivals, because its changing shape is easy to see and count; the word "month" comes from "moon".
exercise: How long is it from one full moon to the next? | About 29 and a half days | Exactly 7 days, one for each day of the week | About 365 days, the same as a whole year | Exactly 24 hours, one whole day and night
exercise: Isla sees a full moon on 3 March. When is the next one? | Around 1 or 2 April | Around 10 March | Around 3 May | Around 3 March next year
exercise: Why can't we see the Moon at new moon? | Its lit side faces away from us | The Earth's shadow covers it | It hides behind the Sun for a week | It switches off its own light
exercise: What is the Moon doing when it is waxing? | Looking bigger each night | Looking smaller each night | Staying the same size | Turning a different colour
exercise: Where does moonlight come from? | Sunlight bouncing off the Moon | Light made deep inside the Moon | Light from all the stars around it | Fires burning on the Moon's surface
exercise: What gives us a year? | One trip of the Earth round the Sun | One spin of the Earth | One trip of the Moon round the Earth | One spin of the Sun
exercise: Noah is 10. How many times has he gone round the Sun? | 10 | 1 | 120 | 3,650
exercise: About how long is one trip of the Earth round the Sun? | About 940 million kilometres | About 940 kilometres | About 40,000 kilometres | About 384,000 kilometres
exercise: How many full moons are there in most years? | 12 | 4 | 52 | 365
exercise: What is a "blue moon"? | An extra full moon that isn't really blue | A Moon that shines bright blue after a rainy night | The Moon during an eclipse | A new moon in winter
exercise: What is a supermoon? | A full moon when the Moon is nearest us | A full moon that looks twice as big as usual | A Moon that has really grown bigger in size | Two full moons in the sky at the same time
exercise: About how much later does the Moon rise each night? | About 50 minutes | About 5 minutes | About 12 hours | It rises at the same time
exercise: Why does the Moon rise later each night? | It moves along its path, so the Earth must turn more to catch it | The Moon spins more slowly every night | The Earth spins more slowly at night | The Sun pushes the Moon back a little bit further each night as it sets
exercise: About how many days do 12 Moon months make? | About 354 | About 365 | About 300 | About 400
exercise: Why do we always see the same face of the Moon? | It spins once in the same time it goes round us | It does not spin at all | The other side is always dark | The Earth blocks our view of the other side
exercise: What did people long ago use the Moon for? | Counting months for farming and festivals | Telling the exact minute of the day, like a clock | Measuring the length of a year exactly, to the hour | Knowing for sure when it would rain the next day
test: Ruby: "Moon's gone, it's hiding, innit." What's really happening? | It's new moon: the lit side faces away from us | The Moon has gone behind the Sun for good | The Earth's shadow is covering the whole Moon | The Moon has left for the other side of the Earth for a month
test: Sam's teacher asks how long the Earth takes to go round the Sun. Sam: "A day, easy." His mate whispers, "Nah, you muppet." What's right? | About 365 and a quarter days; a day is one spin | One day, so Sam is right | About one month, like the Moon | About 29 and a half days, the same as one Moon month
test: Grandad says "Happy trip round the Sun!" on Noah's 10th birthday. Why? | Each birthday marks one more full orbit of the Sun | Noah went to space on a school trip | The Sun goes round Noah on his birthday | Birthdays are the days when the Moon goes once round the Sun
test: Holly's cousin from Boston: "We had a blue moon, it was wicked cool!" ("Wicked" means very, in New England.) What did she really see? | An extra full moon that looked its normal colour | A full moon that was bright blue | A Moon with a blue ring of rain round it | A new moon in the middle of summer
test: Ben's mum: "Supermoon tonight, it's gonna be massive!" Ben looks: "Mid." ("Mid" means nothing special.) Why? | It's only a little bigger, which is hard to notice | Supermoons only look big from space, not from the ground | Supermoons are actually smaller than normal full moons | Clouds always cover a supermoon, so you never see it
test: Liam sees the Moon rise at 7 pm on Monday. About when will it rise on Tuesday? | About 7:50 pm | About 7 pm | About 6:10 pm | About 7 am
test: Erin: "Moon's out at lunchtime, isn't that the night shift?" What's true? | The Moon is up about 12 hours a day, and those hours move round the clock | The Moon only comes out at night, so it must be a cloud | It's a different, smaller moon that comes out by day | The Moon and the Sun swap places at noon
test: What would happen to a calendar made only of 12 Moon months, after 3 years? | Its New Year would come about 33 days earlier in the seasons | Its New Year would land on exactly the same day as in the first year | Its New Year would come about 3 days later | It would fall a whole year behind
test: Aria's big brother, "our kid" as they say in Manchester, reckons the Moon doesn't spin at all. Is he right? | No, it spins once for each trip round us, so one face stays towards us | Yes, it never spins, so we always see one side | Yes, and the far side is always in the dark | No, it spins once a day like the Earth, so over a day we get to see all of it
test: In a cartoon, a wolf howls at a full moon every night of the month. What's wrong? | A full moon comes only about once every 29 and a half days | Nothing, the Moon is full every night | The wolf's howling makes the Moon full | The Moon is full only once a year
test: Grace's teacher: "Farmers long ago counted moons." Why the Moon, not the Sun? | Its changing shape is easy to see and count | The Sun did not rise every day in those days | The Moon tells you the exact hour of the day | The Moon decides when the crops grow and ripen
exam: Identify the principal cause of the phases of the Moon. | The varying portion of its sunlit half visible from the Earth | The shadow cast upon it by the Earth each night | The varying quantity of light produced within the Moon | The partial obscuring of the Moon by clouds
exam: Given that a full moon is observed upon the third of March, determine the approximate date of the succeeding full moon. | The first or second of April | The tenth of March | The third of May | The third of March of the following year
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the interval designated "a year". | One revolution of the Earth about the Sun, approximately 365 and a quarter days | One rotation of the Earth upon its axis | Twelve revolutions of the Moon, amounting to exactly 365 days | The interval between two successive full moons
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the circumstance whereby the same face of the Moon is perpetually presented to the Earth? | The Moon rotates once in the period of one revolution about the Earth | The Moon does not rotate at all | The far side of the Moon is perpetually unlit | The Earth perpetually obstructs all observation of the Moon's far side
